          "Confined to the territorial limits of the State/UT"
        ],
        [
          "Nature of Remedy",
          "Mandatory obligation to grant relief",
          "Discretionary remedy by the court"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "f04b8yv"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mce10ey",
      "content": "• Judges retire at age 62."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "srysl0u",
      "content": "• They can resign to the President."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b6j6m7n",
      "content": "• They can be removed through impeachment."
    },
    {
      "id": "p0ntd1g",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Jurisdiction of High Court"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v8m4i86",
      "content": "Original, Appellate, Writ, and Supervisory jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "id": "h2cg82u",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Types of Writs"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "High Court's writ jurisdiction under Article 226 is wider than the Supreme Court's under Article 32, as it covers both Fundamental Rights and ordinary legal rights.",
        "The retirement age of High Court judges was increased from 60 to 62 years by the 15th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1963.",
        "A judge of a High Court can be removed from office in the same manner and on the same grounds as a Supreme Court judge."
      ],
      "id": "fpljhk0"
    },
